{"text": "December 4, 2017\n\nThe Honorable Ken Cooley\nChair, Assembly Rules Committee\nState Capitol Room\nSacramento, CA 95814\n\nDear Assembly Member Cooley,\nlam writing to report that | was \u2018sexually assaulted by Assembly Member Dababneh.\n\n{tis not my intention to report every detail of what happened in this letter, but here is a brief\ndescription,\n\n\u2018On January 16, 2016, Mr. Dababneh and | were two of the many invited guests at a Party to\ncelebrate two mutual friends who were planning to get married. Most of the guests in\nattendance were political professionals, including many colleagues and associates | have known\nfor years. | should have had no reason to fear for my safety. When I went to the bathroom after\nbeing at the party for a few hours, | felt the weight of a body push me into the restroom. |\nheard the door slam behind us. When | turned around, | saw that it was Matt Dababneh. He\nstood blocking the door and began to masturbate and move toward me, urging me to touch\nhim. It was a terrifying experience. During the time he blocked me in that room, my instincts\nwere focused on escaping without any physical contact and in a way that would not cause a\nscene. Before the ordeal ended, he told me not to tell anyone.\n\nAlthough a number of press reports have placed this event in Sacramento, it actually occurred\nat a private venue inside a Las Vegas Hotel. ! went along with the assumption that it happened\nin Sacramento because | realized that correcting this assumption would likely have led to the\nidentification of Mr. Dababneh and | was not ready to take this step. | wish | could have\nCorrected this error sooner, but until | heard directly from the members of the Assembly\nSubcommittee during the hearing held last week that they are committed to protecting vi\nIwas not ready to name the perpetrator.\n\nFor nearly two years, | have lived in fear that | will be shunned or retaliated against if my\nProfessional community learns that | was sexually assaulted by a lawmaker. And that fear\ncaused me to remain silent, instead of \u2018seeking justice through formal channels in the\nlegislature. My fear comes not only from the perpetrator\u2019s instruction not to tell, but also from\nthe lack of a trustworthy system in the state house for investigating reports of sexual\nharassment and holding those who commit it accountable, regardless of their position.\n\nAt the November 28\" hearing held by the Assembly Rules Subcommittee on Harassment,\nDiscrimination, and Retaliation Prevention and Response, you encouraged women to step\nforward with a promise that perpetrators would be held accountable. | still fear that | will be\nretaliated against. But | am stepping forward. Victims stepping forward deserve protection from",
